.Dd August 24, 2026
.Dt ACPI_FAN 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm acpi_fan
.Nd ACPI fan device driver
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Cd "device acpi"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
driver attaches to ACPI fan devices, that is, devices with a hardware ID
of
.Li PNP0C0B ,
and exports their speed and state through
.Xr sysctl 8 .
.Pp
Only fans that implement the ACPI 4.0 fan control interface are supported.
The driver requires the
.Li _FIF
control method to be present and does not attach without it, so fans that
are driven purely by the platform through a thermal zone's active cooling
list are not claimed.
.Pp
Fans are numbered sequentially in the order they appear in the AML, and each
one gets its own
.Va dev.fan.%d
sysctl tree.
.Pp
A fan supports one of two control models, as reported by the
.Li FineGrainControl
field of
.Li _FIF :
.Bl -bullet
.It
With fine grain control, any level from 0 to 100 may be requested, and the
firmware interprets it as a percentage of maximum speed.
.It
Without fine grain control, only the discrete levels the firmware enumerates
in its
.Li _FPS
performance state table may be requested.
.El
.Pp
The
.Va fine_grind
sysctl reports which model is in use, and the set of sysctls the driver
creates differs accordingly.
.Sh SYSCTL VARIABLES
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Va dev.fan.%d.fine_grind
Whether the fan supports fine grain control.
Read-only.
.It Va dev.fan.%d.set_level
The level last requested through this sysctl.
Writing to it calls the firmware's
.Li _FSL
method to change the fan's speed.
.Pp
For a fine grain fan the value is a percentage of maximum speed, from 0 to
100; values outside that range are rejected with
.Er EINVAL .
The value is rounded down to a multiple of
.Va steps
before being passed to the firmware.
.Pp
Otherwise the value must be one of the control values listed by
.Va levels ,
and any other value is rejected with
.Er EINVAL .
.Pp
This is also a loader tunable, so an initial level may be set from
.Xr loader.conf 5 .
.It Va dev.fan.%d.status
The fan's current control level and speed in revolutions per minute, read
from the firmware's
.Li _FST
method at the time the sysctl is read.
Read-only.
.Pp
A speed of 4294967295
.Pq Li 0xffffffff
means the platform does not report the fan's speed.
.It Va dev.fan.%d.steps
The step size recommended by the firmware for
.Va set_level ,
in percent.
Present only when
.Va fine_grind
is 1.
Read-only.
.It Va dev.fan.%d.levels
The fan's performance states, one per line, as enumerated by the firmware's
.Li _FPS
method.
Present only when
.Va fine_grind
is 0.
Read-only.
.Pp
Each line reports the control value to write to
.Va set_level ,
the active cooling trip point associated with the state, the expected speed
in revolutions per minute, the expected noise level in centibels, and the
expected power draw in milliwatts.
Any of these fields may be 4294967295
.Pq Li 0xffffffff ,
which means the firmware does not provide that value.
.El
.Sh LOADER TUNABLES
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Va debug.acpi.disabled
Adding
.Dq Li fan
to this list prevents
.Nm
from attaching to any device.
See
.Xr acpi 4 .
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
List the fans present on the system:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
% sysctl -N dev.fan | cut -d. -f1-3 | sort -u
dev.fan.0
.Ed
.Pp
Show a fan's control model and current state:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
% sysctl dev.fan.0.fine_grind dev.fan.0.status
dev.fan.0.fine_grind: 0
dev.fan.0.status: Control Level: 50, Speed: 2400 RPM
.Ed
.Pp
Since this fan does not support fine grain control, ask the firmware which
levels it accepts, then select one:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
% sysctl dev.fan.0.levels
dev.fan.0.levels: Ctrl: 0, TripPoint: 4294967295, Speed: 0, NoiseLevel: 0, Power: 0
Ctrl: 50, TripPoint: 4294967295, Speed: 2400, NoiseLevel: 300, Power: 1500
Ctrl: 100, TripPoint: 4294967295, Speed: 4800, NoiseLevel: 500, Power: 3000
# sysctl dev.fan.0.set_level=100
dev.fan.0.set_level: 50 -> 100
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr acpi 4 ,
.Xr acpi_thermal 4 ,
.Xr loader.conf 5 ,
.Xr acpidump 8 ,
.Xr sysctl 8
.Sh HISTORY
The
.Nm
driver first appeared in
.Fx 16.0 .
.Sh AUTHORS
.An -nosplit
The
.Nm
driver and this manual page were written by
.An ShengYi Hung Aq Mt aokblast@FreeBSD.org .
.Sh CAVEATS
The fan is shared with the platform.
Firmware and the ACPI thermal zones described in
.Xr acpi_thermal 4
change the fan's speed on their own, so the value of
.Va set_level
is only the level this driver last requested, not necessarily the level the
fan is running at.
Read
.Va status
for that.
.Pp
Across a suspend and resume cycle the driver records the fan's level, stops
the fan, and restores the recorded level on resume.
Restoring is best effort: a firmware that rejects the request is not treated
as a resume failure.
